# ==============================================================================
# .gitignore: FILES THAT GIT SHOULD NOT TRACK
# ==============================================================================
#
# What is this file?
#   Git keeps a history of the files in this project and uploads them to
#   GitHub when you push. Some files must NOT go there: files with secret keys,
#   and files that programs create automatically (which are big, change all the
#   time, and can be re-created at any time). This file lists them. Git acts
#   as if those files didn't exist: "git status" doesn't show them, and
#   "git add" skips them.
#
# How to read it:
#   - Each line that doesn't start with # is a pattern: a file or folder name,
#     sometimes with special characters. Git ignores every file or folder whose
#     name matches a pattern.
#   - Lines that start with # are notes for people, like this one.
#   - A * matches any characters, any number of them (also none), within a
#     single file or folder name. For example, *.pem matches key.pem and
#     cert.pem, and npm-debug.log* matches npm-debug.log and
#     npm-debug.log.12345.
#   - A / at the START means "only in the project's top folder". /node_modules
#     ignores the node_modules folder at the top, but not a folder of the same
#     name deeper inside. Without it, the pattern matches at any depth.
#   - A / at the END means "only folders, not files", for example /.next/.
#
# Two things that surprise people:
#   - .gitignore only affects files that Git isn't tracking yet. If a file was
#     already committed, adding its name here doesn't remove it from Git or
#     from GitHub. (To stop tracking it: git rm --cached FILENAME.)
#   - Ignored files still exist on your computer, and the app still uses them.
#     They are only kept out of Git and GitHub.
#
# This file itself IS tracked, so everyone who copies the project gets the
# same rules. The supabase folder has its own .gitignore, with rules for the
# files inside that folder.
# ==============================================================================

# --- Packages -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The code libraries that "npm install" downloads. Thousands of files; anyone
# can re-create them with "npm install", so they are never uploaded.
/node_modules
# Files of Yarn, another package manager. This project uses npm, but the
# patterns do no harm.
/.pnp
.pnp.js
.yarn/install-state.gz

# --- Test reports -------------------------------------------------------------
# Reports that the automated tests can create.
/coverage

# --- The built app ------------------------------------------------------------
# Next.js creates these when you run or build the app (npm run dev, npm run
# build). They are re-created each time.
/.next/
/out/
/build

# --- Secret keys and settings -------------------------------------------------
# .env*.local matches every file whose name starts with .env and ends with
# .local, with anything (or nothing) in between: .env.local,
# .env.test.local, .env.development.local, .env.production.local.
# These hold your own values and secret keys. They do NOT match the templates
# .env.example and .env.test.example, which hold only fake values and are
# meant to be on GitHub.
.env*.local
# A plain .env file (some tutorials use one) can hold secret keys too.
.env
# Files ending in .pem hold private keys or certificates, for example key.pem.
*.pem

# --- Files that tools create on your computer ---------------------------------
# The Mac's Finder creates .DS_Store files in folders you open.
.DS_Store
# Error logs that npm and Yarn write when something goes wrong.
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
# The Vercel command-line tool's link to your Vercel project.
.vercel
# TypeScript's build cache, and a file that Next.js writes for TypeScript.
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# --- Scratch folder -----------------------------------------------------------
# For temporary files you don't want in Git.
_tmp/
